        "text": "{\"requestId\":\"43b786b3ccc184511f57b120420d7f23\",\"resolvedSearchType\":\"\",\"results\":[{\"id\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/experience/amazon/amazon-sde2-interview-experience-full-loop\",\"url\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/experience/amazon/amazon-sde2-interview-experience-full-loop\",\"publishedDate\":\"2026-02-13T00:00:00.000Z\",\"author\":\"Interview Experiences\",\"text\":\"Amazon Sde2 (full Loop) - Amazon | Interview Experiences\\n\\n# Amazon SDE2 Interview Experience (Full Loop)\\n\\n· SDE II\\n\\nFebruary 13, 2026 · 368 reads\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nI recently completed a full-loop SDE2 interview at Amazon, which included rounds on data structures and algorithms, low-level design, system design, and behavioral questions based on Leadership Principles. I am currently awaiting the debrief results from HR.\\n\\n## Full Experience\\n\\nHi everyone,\\n\\nI recently appeared for the Amazon SDE2 interview and completed the full loop. Sharing my experience below:\\n\\n#### Round 1: DSA\\n\\nProblem: Minimum Number of Days to Disconnect Island https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-days-to-disconnect-island/description/\\n\\nI was not able to complete the code for all test cases.\\n\\nSelf Rating: Lean Hire\\n\\n#### Round 2: Bar Raiser\\n\\nDiscussion:\\n\\n- Leadership Principles (LP)\\n- Design a service to find a rider for a quick commerce app-LLD\\n\\nSelf Rating: Hire\\n\\n#### Round 3: LLD (Low-Level Design)\\n\\nProblem: Design an offline download manager for a social media app.\\n\\nCompleted the code and answered follow-up questions.\\n\\nSelf Rating: Hire\\n\\n#### Round 4: Hiring Manager\\n\\nDiscussion:\\n\\n- Leadership Principles (LP)\\n- Design an Event Logging System\\n\\nAnswered follow-up questions and completed the design.\\n\\nSelf Rating: Hire\\n\\n#### Current Status\\n\\nI called HR today and was informed that the debrief is scheduled for this evening. I tried calling again twice after 6 PM but didn’t get a response.\\n\\nNot sure what to expect , maybe I’m overthinking. I just wish HRs would share updates promptly, whatever the result may be.\\n\\nWill update once I hear back.\\n\\n## Interview Questions (6)\\n\\n1.\\n\\n### Minimum Number of Days to Disconnect Island\\n\\nData Structures & Algorithms\\n\\nThe problem asks for the minimum number of days to disconnect an island. The full problem description is available via the provided LeetCode link.\\n\\n2.\\n\\n### Amazon Leadership Principles Discussion\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nDiscussion centered around Amazon's Leadership Principles, covering behavioral aspects and past experiences relevant to these principles.\\n\\n3.\\n\\n### Design a Rider-Finding Service (LLD)\\n\\nSystem Design\\n\\nLow-level design of a service to efficiently find a rider for a quick commerce application.\\n\\n4.\\n\\n### Design an Offline Download Manager\\n\\nSystem Design\\n\\nDesign an offline download manager for a social media application, including its architecture and core functionalities.\\n\\n5.\\n\\n### Amazon Leadership Principles Discussion\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nDiscussion centered around Amazon's Leadership Principles, covering behavioral aspects and past experiences relevant to these principles.\\n\\n6.\\n\\n### Design an Event Logging System\\n\\nDesign an event logging system, considering aspects like scalability, reliability, and data processing.\\n\\nDiscussion (0)\\n\\n### No comments yet\\n\\n- raunit@raunit.dev\\n- Built to help engineers prepare for interviews.\",\"image\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/opengraph-image\",\"title\":\"Amazon Sde2 (full Loop) - Amazon | Interview Experiences\"},{\"id\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/experience/amazon/amazon-sde-2-interview-experience-jan-feb-2026-rejected\",\"url\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/experience/amazon/amazon-sde-2-interview-experience-jan-feb-2026-rejected\",\"publishedDate\":\"2026-02-12T00:00:00.000Z\",\"author\":\"Interview Experiences\",\"text\":\"Amazon Sde 2 [jan-feb 2026] [rej - Amazon | Interview Experiences\\n\\n### Help the Community\\n\\nShare your interview experience to help others prepare!\\n\\n### Get the App\\n\\nPractice on the go! Download our Android app to read experiences anywhere.\\n\\n### Amazon\\n\\nMore Experiences\\n\\nRecent from amazon\\n\\nAmazon | AWS | SDE2 roleSDE II•369 reads\\n\\n🚀 Amazon SDE Interview Experience (4 Rounds)SDE I•405 reads\\n\\nAmazon SDE Intern 2026 Interview Exp (Oncampus)SDE Intern•562 reads\\n\\n### Recent Experiences\\n\\n[Deloitte OA] 7th Nearest Palindrome | Optimal C++ Solution | Precompute + Binary SearchAnalyst•297 reads\\n\\nSalesforce AMTS Interview || Should I move?AMTS•510 reads\\n\\nPlaud.AI Backend Engineer Interview ExperienceBackend Engineer•317 reads\\n\\nWalmart Interview Exp - Senior software engineerSenior Software Engineer•389 reads\\n\\nTwilio | Senior Software Engineer | Dublin, IrelandSenior Software Engineer•298 reads\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nI applied for an SDE 2 position at Amazon and went through multiple interview rounds including an Online Assessment, Low-Level Design, Data Structures & Algorithms, and High-Level Design. Despite some positive performances, I was ultimately rejected after the third round, with feedback indicating concerns in the LLD part and the importance of HLD.\\n\\n## Full Experience\\n\\nI applied for SDE 2 position. A day after, I recieved the OA test link and a week later HR reached out to me for interview loops. HR mentioned that each round will be eliminatory\\n\\nRound 0: OA 1. A question based on binary search and greedy. Not a standard leetcode que\\n\\nSign in to read the complete interview experience\\n\\n## Interview Questions (12)\\n\\n1.\\n\\n### Behavioral: Disagree and Commit\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nTell me about a time when you disagreed with your manager or tech lead, and how you handled it.\\n\\n2.\\n\\n### Behavioral: Fixing Recurring Issues\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nDescribe a situation where you identified and fixed a recurring issue. How did you ensure it wouldn't happen again?\\n\\n3.\\n\\n### Behavioral: Quality vs. Deadline\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nHow do you balance quality of work with meeting deadlines?\\n\\n4.\\n\\n### Design Notification Service (SMS + Email)\\n\\nSystem Design\\n\\nDesign a Notification Service that handles SMS and Email notifications. It should receive updates from a ParcelDeliveryService when a parcel goes out for delivery to a customer, utilize existing EmailService and SMSService which can send notifications directly with a rate limit of 5000/min, be able to resend notifications if sending failed, and log all communications sent to the user. A lot of follow-up questions were asked.\\n\\n5.\\n\\n### Behavioral: Delivering Results Quickly\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nDescribe a time when you delivered a feature or completed work in a short amount of time. I had some follow-up questions when I discussed a customer purge feature.\\n\\n6.\\n\\n### Behavioral: Learning and Being Curious (Feature Pivot)\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nTell me about a time when you had to pivot the direction of a feature after having developed 75% of it, demonstrating your ability to learn and be curious.\\n\\n7.\\n\\n### Jump Game I\\n\\nData Structures & Algorithms·Medium\\n\\nGiven an array of non-negative integers nums, you are initially positioned at the first index. Each element in the array represents your maximum jump length at that position. Determine if you can reach the last index.\\n\\n8.\\n\\n### Jump Game II\\n\\nData Structures & Algorithms·Medium\\n\\nGiven an array of non-negative integers nums, you are initially positioned at the first index. Each element in the array represents your maximum jump length at that position. Your goal is to reach the last index in the minimum number of jumps.\\n\\n9.\\n\\n### Jump Game III\\n\\nData Structures & Algorithms·Medium\\n\\nGiven an array of non-negative integers arr, you are initially positioned at start index. When you are at index i, you can jump to i + arr[i] or i - arr[i]. Return true if you can reach any index with value 0, otherwise return false. Notice that you can not jump outside of the array at any time.\\n\\n10.\\n\\n### Behavioral: Uncovering and Solving Team Problems\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nTell me about a time when you uncovered a problem within your team and how you took ownership to solve it.\\n\\n11.\\n\\n### Behavioral: Dealing with Teammate Performance Impact\\n\\nBehavioral\\n\\nDescribe a situation where a teammate's poor performance affected your work or team goals. How did you handle it?\\n\\n12.\\n\\n### Design Twitter (High Scale)\\n\\nSystem Design·Hard\\n\\nDesign Twitter, focusing on handling high throughput: 600k reads per second, 6k writes per second, and 300 million daily active users (DAU).\\n\\nSign in to view all 12 interview questions\\n\\nView All Questions →\\n\\n## Preparation Tips\\n\\nResourced used to prepare for interview:\\n\\n1. Top interview 150 leetcode questions\\n2. HLD from https://www.hellointerview.com/\\n3. LLD from Concept & Coding by Shrayansh youtube channel\\n\\nSuggestions for those going to attend SDE 2 Interview loop:\\n\\n1. It is important to go in depth for HLD and LLD interviews\\n2. Solve 2 DSA Questions in 45 mins, so practise DSA well\\n3. LPs will be discussed for 15-30 mins in each interview. Make sure to have all of them prepared.\\n4. Communicate your thoughts clearly.\\n\\nI saw suggestions like this in some other discussions and subconsciously ignored... now it costed me a SDE 2 job at Amazon (60LPA+ lost :( )\\n\\n📣 Found this helpful? Please share it with friends who are preparing for interviews!\\n\\nWhatsApp Twitter / X LinkedIn Copy Link\\n\\nMore from amazon Browse All Experiences\\n\\nDiscussion (0)\\n\\nShare your thoughts and ask questions\\n\\n### Join the Discussion\\n\\nSign in with Google to share your thoughts and ask questions\\n\\n### No comments yet\\n\\nBe the first to share your thoughts and start the discussion!\",\"image\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/opengraph-image\",\"favicon\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/favicon-32.png\",\"title\":\"Amazon Sde 2 [jan-feb 2026] [rej - Amazon | Interview Experiences\"},{\"id\":\"https://codesnatch.io/community/interview-experiences/amazon-sde2-loop-what-to-expect\",\"url\":\"https://codesnatch.io/community/interview-experiences/amazon-sde2-loop-what-to-expect\",\"publishedDate\":\"2026-02-28T00:00:00.000Z\",\"author\":\"CodeSnatch\",\"text\":\"Amazon SDE-2 Loop: What to Expect | Community | CodeSnatch\\n\\n/\\n\\n# Amazon SDE-2 Loop: What to Expect\\n\\nWhat the Amazon SDE-2 loop actually feels like, round by round, and how the leadership principles thread through every conversation.\\n\\namazon\\n\\nleadership-principles\\n\\nbar-raiser\\n\\ninterview-prep\\n\\nbehavioral\\n\\nBy @sarahwilson\\n\\nFebruary 28, 2026\\n\\n·\\n\\nUpdated August 9, 2026\\n\\n347 views\\n\\n4\\n\\nRate\\n\\nShare\\n\\nI went through an Amazon SDE-2 loop for an AWS-adjacent team last spring. I had read the leadership principles before, the way everyone reads them, but I had not understood until the loop that they are not a behavioral-round add-on. They are the substrate. Every round, including the coding rounds, was scored on at least two leadership principles in addition to the technical signal.\\n\\n### The Shape of the Loop\\n\\nThe full SDE-2 loop in the loops I sat through was five rounds, all in one virtual onsite day:\\n\\n```\\nLoop layout (one day, ~5.5 hours including a 30-minute break)\\n  Round 1   Coding (45 min)\\n  Round 2   System design (60 min)\\n  Round 3   Coding plus behavioral (60 min)\\n  Round 4   Bar raiser (60 min)\\n  Round 5   Hiring manager (45 min)\\n```\\n\\nExpand code\\n\\nEach interviewer was assigned two leadership principles to probe. The recruiter sent me a pre-loop email listing which principles to expect overall, but not who was assigned which. So the safe assumption was that any of them could surface in any round.\\n\\n### Round 1: Coding\\n\\nA medium array problem with a follow-up that turned it into a stream. I solved the base case in about twenty minutes, then walked through how I would adapt the solution if the input arrived one element at a time. The interviewer asked me to estimate the worst-case memory of the streaming version. I did, including a sentence about why the bound was tight.\\n\\nThe behavioral question at the end was about a time I had simplified something. I told a story about removing a service, with the metric attached: the team's on-call pages dropped from roughly three a week to under one a week the month after.\\n\\n### Round 2: System Design\\n\\nDesign a notification system for a retail-style product catalog. The interviewer wanted me to anchor in customer obsession early, which I did by listing three customer-visible signals (delivery latency, duplicate notifications, the cost of a missed notification) before drawing anything. I designed around an event log plus a fan-out worker pool. The interviewer pushed on idempotency for about ten minutes, which is the standard Amazon design-round move in my experience: pick one specific failure mode and grill it.\\n\\n### Round 3: Coding + Behavioral\\n\\nThe coding half was a string problem. The behavioral half had two principles, ownership and dive deep. The dive-deep prompt was: walk me through the most technical thing you have done recently. I picked a query optimization story with the actual`EXPLAIN` output I had screenshotted from the project, and the interviewer kept drilling on it. By the eighth follow-up I was past the rehearsed script and into the actual system, which I think was the point of the exercise.\\n\\n### Round 4: Bar Raiser\\n\\nThe bar raiser round was entirely behavioral. Six stories in sixty minutes, with deep follow-ups on three of them. The bar raiser is from a different org by design, so they had no context on my domain. They were grading raise-the-bar and a second principle I never confirmed.\\n\\nThe story that did the most work for me was a failure: a feature I had shipped that caused a regression we caught two days later. I told it without sanding the rough edges, including the part where the post-incident document had me down as the proximate cause. The bar raiser asked four follow-ups about what I would do differently and then moved on. I think the unsanded version is what the bar raiser wanted, though I cannot prove it.\\n\\n### Round 5: Hiring Manager\\n\\nForty-five minutes, mostly conversation about the team's roadmap and a couple of softer behavioral questions. This round felt the most like a normal job conversation, but my recruiter told me afterward that the hiring manager scores on two principles too.\\n\\n### What Tipped the Decision\\n\\nThe loop ended with an offer. The recruiter shared, on the offer call, that the bar raiser and round three had been the strongest, and round one had been the weakest, but no round had been below bar.\\n\\nI ended up declining. The team was a good match technically but the on-call rotation was heavier than I wanted at that life-stage and the comp band did not stretch to where I needed it to. I tell the full story here because every Amazon offer write-up online ends with sign, and that is not how every loop ends.\\n\\n### The leadership-principles trick that changed how I prep\\n\\nMemorize the leadership principles in your own words, not the official phrasing. Have a story for each, including at least three failure stories. Treat every round as having a hidden behavioral component, including the coding rounds. And do not under-prep the dive-deep prompt: shallow technical stories are the single thing that the bar raiser round in the loops I sat through was most willing to fail you on.\\n\\nBack to Interview Experiences Mark as Read\",\"image\":\"https://codesnatch.io/community/interview-experiences/amazon-sde2-loop-what-to-expect/opengraph-image\",\"favicon\":\"https://codesnatch.io/logo-square-32.png\",\"title\":\"Amazon SDE-2 Loop: What to Expect | Community | CodeSnatch\"},{\"id\":\"https://medium.com/@hissandanish/amazon-sde-2-interview-experience-selected-b1f8b0a69de5\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@hissandanish/amazon-sde-2-interview-experience-selected-b1f8b0a69de5\",\"publishedDate\":\"2026-02-05T00:00:00.000Z\",\"author\":\"Hissan Danish\",\"text\":\"Amazon SDE-2 Interview experience-Selected | by Hissan Danish | Medium\\n\\nOpen in app\\n\\nSign up\\n\\nGet app\\n\\nSign up\\n\\n# Amazon SDE-2 Interview experience-Selected\\n\\n2 min read\\n\\nFeb 5, 2026\\n\\n--\\n\\n1\\n\\nShare\\n\\nThis is for Amazon India SDE-2 (L5) level. The process involved :1. Initial recruiter reach out 2. Online assessment3. DSA-problem solving + Leadership principles4. DSA-problem solving + Leadership principles5. LLD + Leadership principles6. HLD + Hiring manager round\\n\\nRecruiter reached out via linkedin, had an initial call about my work experience and reason for interest in Amazon. Online assessment invite was sent. It is hosted in hackerrank. It consists of 2 coding questions, 1 medium and 1 medium-hard problem [topics : greedy, binary search]\\n\\nAfter I cleared the OA, onsite invites were sent. Intially LLD and DSA-1 round were scheduled. Based on the feedback remaining rounds got scheduled.\\n\\nDSA-1 round : Asked 2 medium leetcode style problems + 2 LPs. [topics : intervals and sweep line based, max meetings scheduling]\\n\\nLLD round : Asked to design token management system for restaurant + 2LPs [topics tested: classes and DB design, handling contention and concurrency, exhaustive list of APIs and psuedo code for few important APIs, focussed on redis usage for atomic operations]\\n\\n## Get Hissan Danish’s stories in your inbox\\n\\nJoin Medium for free to get updates from this writer.\\n\\nSubscribe\\n\\nSubscribe\\n\\nRemember me for faster sign in\\n\\nDSA-2 round: Asked 1 medium-hard leetcode problem + 2LPs+ asked to explain the problem solved in OA. [topics: Trapping rain water variant ]\\n\\nHLD+HM round : Asked to design LMS system for companies and discussion around previous project and experiences. [focus areas: requirement gathering, basic design, deep dives on DB choice, handling search using elastic search, presigned URLs for better upload/download of content, security aspects and persistent connections for tracking progress]\\n\\nAll the rounds were scheduled for 1 hr. But almost all the onsite rounds got stretched by 15–20mins for further discussion. Emphasis is not on getting perfect or most efficient solution but coming up with a correct solution and communicating the trade offs properly. Leadership principles are equally important, answered them using STAR technique.\\n\\nEntire process got completed within a week and got the offer after 3 days of last interview. Recuiter was extremely helpful throughout the process.\\n\\nLet me know if you have any questionsHappy learning and stay curious\\n\\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\\n\\nSystem Design Interview\\n\\n## Written by Hissan Danish\\n\\nSoftware Engineer | DSA Mentor | Amateur writer | Philosophy and Neuroscience | Life experiences and Learnings\\n\\n## Responses (1)\\n\\n```\\nHi ,Thanks for the amazing article, how to write code in onsite round ? pen & paper or carry our laptop.\\n```\\n\\n--\\n\\nReply\\n\\n## More from Hissan Danish\\n\\n·\\n\\nFeb 5\\n\\n## Goldman Sachs Associate Software Engineer Interview Experience — Selected\\n\\n### My interview experience with Goldman Sachs for the SDE-2 (Associate Software Engineer) role:\\n\\n·\\n\\nFeb 5\\n\\n## Goldman Sachs Associate Software Engineer Interview Experience — Selected\\n\\n### My interview experience with Goldman Sachs for the SDE-2 (Associate Software Engineer) role:\\n\\nSee all from Hissan Danish\\n\\n## Recommended from Medium\\n\\nIn\\n\\nby\\n\\n·\\n\\nMay 4\\n\\n## Airbnb Interview Process\\n\\n### Note: The interview process might change over the course of time.\\n\\nIn\\n\\nby\\n\\n·\\n\\nJun 9\\n\\n## DSA Day 93/250: K Closest Points to Origin (LeetCode 973)\\n\\n### Find the k closest points to the origin efficiently using a max heap.\\n\\nMd Johirul Islam\\n\\n·\\n\\nMay 6\\n\\n## Google Interview Mastery — Fenwick Tree (Binary Indexed Tree): The Most Intuitive Guide (with…\\n\\n### If you've ever needed to do these two things many times on an array:\\n\\n·\\n\\nMay 22\\n\\n## 22 Scenario-Based System Design Questions That Reveal How Real Systems Actually Break\\n\\n### Every developer eventually reaches a point where learning syntax, frameworks, and APIs is no longer enough.\\n\\nIn\\n\\nby\\n\\n·\\n\\nApr 13\\n\\n## Every System Design Question I Was Asked in Real Interviews at 10+ Companies\\n\\n### 10 real system design questions from interviews I actually took — including the ones I bombed.\\n\\nA response icon7\\n\\n·\\n\\nJan 1\\n\\n## Idempotency in System Design: How a Single Click Cost Us Twice\\n\\n### The button said “Pay Now.”\\n\\nSee more recommendations\\n\\nText to speech\",\"image\":\"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1200/1*bUZrAmbVF1-wemtQwmw3xA.jpeg\",\"title\":\"Amazon SDE-2 Interview experience-Selected | by Hissan Danish | Medium\"},{\"id\":\"https://leetcode.com/discuss/post/7576550/amazon-sde2-interview-experience-full-lo-jhtv/\",\"url\":\"https://leetcode.com/discuss/post/7576550/amazon-sde2-interview-experience-full-lo-jhtv/\",\"publishedDate\":\"2026-02-13T00:00:00.000Z\",\"text\":\"Amazon SDE2 Interview Experience (Full Loop) - Discuss - LeetCode\\n\\nFor youFor you\\n\\nMost Votes\\n\\nAll Time\\n\\nNewest\\n\\nNo search result\\n\\nAmazon SDE2 Interview Experience (Full Loop)\\n\\nAnonymous User\\n\\n2317\\n\\nHi everyone,\\n\\nI recently appeared for the Amazon SDE2 interview and completed the full loop. Sharing my experience below:\\n\\n⸻\\n\\nRound 1: DSA\\n\\nProblem: Minimum Number of Days to Disconnect Island https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-number-of-days-to-disconnect-island/description/\\n\\nI was not able to complete the code for all test cases.\\n\\nSelf Rating: Lean Hire\\n\\n⸻\\n\\nRound 2: Bar Raiser\\n\\nDiscussion: • Leadership Principles (LP) • Design a service to find a rider for a quick commerce app-LLD\\n\\nSelf Rating: Hire\\n\\n⸻\\n\\nRound 3: LLD (Low-Level Design)\\n\\nProblem: Design an offline download manager for a social media app.\\n\\nCompleted the code and answered follow-up questions.\\n\\nSelf Rating: Hire\\n\\n⸻\\n\\nRound 4: Hiring Manager\\n\\nDiscussion: • Leadership Principles (LP) • Design an Event Logging System\\n\\nAnswered follow-up questions and completed the design.\\n\\nSelf Rating: Hire\\n\\n⸻\\n\\nCurrent Status\\n\\nI called HR today and was informed that the debrief is scheduled for this evening. I tried calling again twice after 6 PM but didn’t get a response.\\n\\nNot sure what to expect , maybe I’m overthinking. I just wish HRs would share updates promptly, whatever the result may be.\\n\\nWill update once I hear back.\\n\\n7\\n\\n11\\n\\nComments (11)\\n\\nSort by:Best\\n\\nComment\\n\\nExplore\\n\\nMore\\n\\nCopyright © 2026 LeetCode\\n\\nUnited States\",\"image\":\"https://leetcode.com/static/images/LeetCode_Sharing.png\",\"title\":\"Amazon SDE2 Interview Experience (Full Loop) - Discuss - LeetCode\"},{\"id\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/experience/amazon/l5-sde-2-amazon-interview-experience-nit-28-yoe\",\"url\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/experience/amazon/l5-sde-2-amazon-interview-experience-nit-28-yoe\",\"publishedDate\":\"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z\",\"author\":\"Interview Experiences\",\"text\":\"L5 (sde-2) | Amazon | | Nit | 2. - Amazon | Interview Experiences\\n\\n# L5 (SDE-2) | Amazon | Interview experience | NIT | 2.8 YOE\\n\\n· SDE-2 (L5)· Hyderabad· 2.8y exp\\n\\nFebruary 22, 2026 · 262 reads\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nI recently went through the full loop for SDE-2 (L5) at Amazon in Hyderabad, which included rounds on HLD, LLD, DSA, and Leadership Principles, and successfully received an offer.\\n\\n## 🚀 Amazon SDE-2 (L5) Interview Experience – Hyderabad\\n\\nRecently went through the full loop for SDE-2 (L5) at Amazon, and wanted to share the real takeaways — especially for folks with <3 years of experience.\\n\\n### Background\\n\\n~2.8 YOE\\n\\nAlready SDE-2 at current company Recruiter reached out and mentioned clearly: “You’ll need to convince the panel given your experience.”\\n\\n### R0 – Online Assessment\\n\\n2 DSA problems (standard elimination). Nothing fancy. Greedy and graph problems.\\n\\n### R1 – HLD + Leadership Principles\\n\\nProblem: Design a Shuttle / Ride Matching Service\\n\\n- Employees register pickup/drop\\n- Time-slot based matching\\n- Notifications to users\\n\\nI designed it as:\\n\\n- Matching service\\n- Event-driven notifications\\n- Async processing (Kafka-style)\\n- Clear separation between booking, matching, and notification layers\\n\\nStrong Hire.\\n\\n### R2 – Bar Raiser / Managerial + DSA\\n\\nResume deep dive 2 Leadership Principles\\n\\nOne DSA question convert integer to Roman String\\n\\n### R3 – DSA Round\\n\\n1. Binary search on answer type problem\\n2. Modified Dijkstra / graph variation\\n\\nFocus was on:\\n\\n- Clean implementation\\n- Edge case handling\\n- Time complexity discussion\\n\\n### R4 – LLD + LPs\\n\\nDesign a PubSub system with configuration. Expected:\\n\\n- Entities\\n- States\\n- Contracts\\n- Extensibility\\n- Throughput upto 10k/second and hence multi-threading & async in Java helped \\n- Used Excalidraw to structure the design clearly.\\n\\n### Fun part \\n\\nI got the offer :p\\n\\n## Interview Questions (3)\\n\\n1.\\n\\n### Design a Shuttle / Ride Matching Service\\n\\nSystem Design\\n\\nDesign a Shuttle / Ride Matching Service with features like employees registering for pickup/drop, time-slot based matching, and user notifications.\\n\\n2.\\n\\n### Integer to Roman String Conversion\\n\\nData Structures & Algorithms· Medium\\n\\nConvert an integer to its Roman numeral string representation.\\n\\n3.\\n\\n### Design a PubSub System\\n\\nSystem Design· Hard\\n\\nDesign a PubSub system with configuration. Expected considerations included entities, states, contracts, extensibility, and handling throughput up to 10k/second.\\n\\n📣 Found this helpful? Please share it with friends who are preparing for interviews!\\n\\nDiscussion (0)\\n\\nShare your thoughts and ask questions\\n\\n### No comments yet\\n\\n- raunit@raunit.dev\\n- Built to help engineers prepare for interviews.\",\"image\":\"https://interviewexperiences.in/opengraph-image\",\"title\":\"L5 (sde-2) | Amazon | | Nit | 2. - Amazon | Interview Experiences\"},{\"id\":\"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/techpranjal_amazoninterview-sde2-systemdesign-activity-7432694576089354240-tiU0\",\"url\":\"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/techpranjal_amazoninterview-sde2-systemdesign-activity-7432694576089354240-tiU0\",\"publishedDate\":\"2026-02-26T00:00:00.000Z\",\"author\":\"Pranjal Jain\",\"text\":\"# From PayPal SE2 to Amazon L5 (SDE-2): The Blueprint of a Successful Transition | Pranjal Jain · LinkedIn · 2026-02-26\\n\\n**Pranjal Jain**: Founder at Prepflix for 1 year 7 months with 5 years 1 month of total professional experience. Previous roles include Instructor at Prepleaf by Masai, Software Developer at Microsoft, and Course Instructor at Imarticus Learning. Based in Delhi, India [IN].\\n\\n---\\n\\nFrom PayPal SE2 to Amazon L5 (SDE-2): The Blueprint of a Successful Transition\\n\\nCracking an L5 (SDE-2) role at Amazon requires more than just high-level coding; it requires a fundamental shift in how you approach problem-solving and leadership. I’m incredibly proud to share the journey of one of our students who recently made the transition from PayPal to Amazon. 🚀\\n\\nThe journey wasn't easy. The Amazon interview process is designed to find the \\\"Bar Raisers,\\\" and here is exactly what it looked like for him:\\nThe Technical Bar: From \\\"LeetCode Hard\\\" problems in the Online Assessment to designing Stack Overflow in an LLD round and a LeetCode-style platform in the HLD round.\\n\\nThe Behavioral Bar: Every single round—from Coding to the Bar Raiser—was anchored in Amazon’s Leadership Principles.\\n\\nThe Strategy: He didn't just solve problems; he mastered the STAR format and prepared at least four specific scenarios for every principle, focusing on \\\"Ownership,\\\" \\\"Dive Deep,\\\" and \\\"Customer Obsession.\\\"\\n\\nAt Prepflix, we don’t believe in \\\"guessing.\\\" We believe in a structured, high-accountability roadmap. This student succeeded because he balanced deep technical preparation in System Design (HLD/LLD) with the communication skills needed to pass a Bar Raiser.\\n\\nThis is the same methodology that has helped 1,000+ engineers break into companies like Google, Amazon, and Microsoft with an average 350% salary jump. As an IIT Kanpur Gold Medalist and Ex-Microsoft SDE, I’ve designed our curriculum to mirror these exact high-stakes environments.\\n\\nAre you ready to stop grinding aimlessly and start preparing with a proven blueprint?\\n\\nThe \\\"Service-Based Trap\\\" is optional. Your growth is inevitable if you have the right strategy.\\n\\nJoin us at prepflix.co.in and let’s land your dream offer. #AmazonInterview #SDE2 #SystemDesign #Prepflix #CareerGrowth #TechHiring #Microsoft #IITKanpur\\n\\n![From PayPal SE2 to Amazon L5 (SDE-2): The Blueprint of a Successful Transition | Pranjal Jain image](https://media.licdn.com/dms/image/v2/D5622AQHiSvVewRdHsQ/feedshare-shrink_800/B56ZyY8VPWKAAg-/0/1772092478929?e=2147483647&v=beta&t=g2BpWNTBjXFArOBG0xQHTtnm5Ec6U5Lw-OSkmFCKCMs)\\n\\n## Engagement\\n\\n- Reactions: 14\",\"image\":\"https://media.licdn.com/dms/image/v2/D5622AQHiSvVewRdHsQ/feedshare-shrink_800/B56ZyY8VPWKAAg-/0/1772092478929?e=2147483647&v=beta&t=g2BpWNTBjXFArOBG0xQHTtnm5Ec6U5Lw-OSkmFCKCMs\",\"title\":\"From PayPal SE2 to Amazon L5 (SDE-2): The Blueprint of a Successful 